# The process model

How harness1's work is to be split across Linux processes, and the mechanisms
each split rests on. The rule this serves is in `research/terminology.dj`: every
harness1 running prompt on a machine shares a single Linux process group. The design here
is a proposal; what exists in the tree is stated as such. The worked precedents
are Kitty, tmux and Herdr; everything read from them is under `## KiTTY` and
`## tmux and Herdr`. The turn model has its own file, `research/turns.dj`, and
with it the omp2 precedent.

## What exists now

Nothing is split. `app/main.zig` prints the system prompt and exits, and the
library's only concurrency is `src/curl.zig` spawning one thread per in-flight
request. `setpgid`, `setsid`, `signalfd`, `waitpid` and `CLOEXEC` appear nowhere
in the tree. So "a running prompt" is a conversation in a single process today, and the
process-group rule holds vacuously because there is one process.

## Roles are not start modes

Two things get conflated when counting processes, and keeping them apart is what
makes the count come out right.

A *role* is what a process is: the server, a running prompt's own child, a client. A
*start mode* is how one came to exist: launched attached to a terminal, detached
from it, or joining a server that is already running. One role can arrive by
several start modes, and a start mode that looks like a separate kind of process
usually is not one.

An running prompt is in neither list. It is a running prompt (`research/terminology.dj`) —
not a process, and not a thread. Which process or thread carries one is
implementation detail, and nothing in this document depends on the answer.

The test is what the process ends up doing, not how it was launched. An
invocation told to detach still becomes the server — that is the server's role
reached by a different start mode. An invocation told to join an existing server
does *not* become one: it is a client.

## An invocation is a client; the server is separate

Running `harness1` does one of two things, and the socket decides which:

- A server is already answering on the socket → the invocation is a *client*.
- Nothing is answering → the invocation *spawns* the server, and then becomes a
  client itself.

Either way the process in front of the user is the client, and the client is the
user-facing side: it draws, reads keys, sends requests, owns the extensions, and
modifies turns through the socket. It is disposable — nothing durable lives only
in it — so killing it loses nothing and reattaching is free, the deal tmux and
Herdr both offer.

The server outlives every client and exits only when all running prompts are done working.
Detaching is therefore not a shutdown; it is the normal case, and the server has
to be started detached (`setsid`, no controlling terminal) so that closing the
terminal does not `SIGHUP` it.

## The server is the database

The server owns the data, in memory and on disk. That is a component of the
server itself, not an external dependency and not a separate process: the working
set is in memory because the running prompt loop reads it constantly, and the durable half
is what lets a restart resume instead of start over.

One writer follows from that. Nothing else opens the store — a client modifies
turns through the socket and the server applies them — so there is no second
party to reconcile with and no lock to argue over.

What the durable half *is* is still open, and it is the first question in
`research/turns.dj`.

## The socket is the lock, the rendezvous, and the liveness test

One pathname unix socket does three jobs, so there is no pid file to go stale:

- *Rendezvous* — clients `connect()` to it.
- *Liveness* — the file existing proves nothing, because a crashed server leaves
  it behind; only a successful `connect()` proves something is answering. That is
  the test: `exists() && connect().is_ok()`.
- *Lock* — the bind is the lock. `bind()` fails with `EADDRINUSE` when the path
  is taken, so the process that binds is the server and every other one is a
  client. A stale file fails the liveness test first, and is unlinked before the
  bind.

Two caveats. An abstract socket has no filesystem entry, so it cannot be the
lock — this needs a pathname socket, which means a runtime directory
(`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`) and a mode that keeps other users out. And
test-then-unlink-then-bind is itself a race when two invocations start at once;
the usual answer is to hold an `O_EXCL` lock file or a `flock()` across the whole
sequence.

## Where the turn model lives

A prompt is every turn visible to the API, so a running prompt's whole state is its
turns, and a running prompt is resumable exactly when those turns are reachable — which
is what makes the client disposable and the server restartable. What a turn is,
and how an abstract turn differs from a raw one, is `research/turns.dj`, and is
not repeated here.

## Mechanisms

### The process group

`setpgid(0, pgid)` in *both* the parent and the child immediately after `fork`,
before `exec` — the double call is what closes the race where the child reaches
`exec` before the parent has set its group. The server creates the group:
`setsid` makes it a session and group leader and detaches from any controlling
terminal; `setpgid(0, 0)` makes a group leader while staying in the session.

A child inherits the group unless it calls `setsid` itself. An running prompt must not, or
the shared group is lost at the first fork.

### Session and terminal

A process whose group is not the terminal's foreground group gets `SIGTTIN` when
it reads the terminal and receives no `SIGINT` from it; `setsid` is stronger
still, and `Ctrl-C` never reaches it. This is what makes the client a separate
group from the fleet: the client is the UI and must be the terminal's foreground
group, while the server and its running prompts are detached from the terminal entirely.
A client is not a running prompt, so the single-group rule is untouched by that.

### File descriptors

`CLOEXEC` on everything except what is deliberately passed, which is the control
socket alone. The better shape is to connect first and hand the *already
connected* fd to the child, rather than a path for it to re-connect to; and never
to pass a listening fd by inheritance, because the backlog comes with it.

### Signals

Mask the handled set in every thread and consume it from one `signalfd` in the
server's loop, so no handler does real work. A workable set is `SIGINT`,
`SIGHUP`, `SIGTERM`, `SIGCHLD`, `SIGUSR1`, `SIGUSR2`. `SIGCHLD` sets a flag and
the loop then calls `waitpid(-1, &status, WNOHANG)` and reports what it reaped. A
signal meant for one running prompt is delivered with `kill(pid, sig)`; one meant for the
machine, with `killpg`.

Between `fork` and `exec` the child must reset every handled signal to `SIG_DFL`
and unblock the mask — a child that inherits the server's blocked set cannot be
signalled normally. Only async-signal-safe calls belong in that window.

### Resource limits

- *cgroup v2* bounds the group's resources: `memory.max` and `pids.max` make the
  kernel's OOM kill take this unit rather than the machine, `memory.events`
  (`oom`, `oom_kill`) is the signal that it happened, `memory.peak` and
  `memory.pressure` (PSI) are the readings to act on before it does, and
  `cgroup.freeze` stops every task in the cgroup atomically — a stronger freeze
  than `SIGSTOP` sent to a process group.
- *`RLIMIT_AS`* per process makes overcommit honest: the allocation fails with a
  real `ENOMEM` instead of the OOM killer arriving later. It counts every mapping
  including thread stacks, so it needs the stack size fixed first.
- *Stack size.* Zig's `Thread.SpawnConfig.default_stack_size` is 16 MiB per
  thread, so 20 concurrent requests reserve roughly 320 MiB of address space
  before a single body buffer. An explicit small stack is a prerequisite for
  either limit above meaning anything.

### Capacity is learned, not declared

There is no fixed cap, and nothing is sized to one. Capacity is what the machine
turns out to hold, discovered by running work until it fails and reading the
error, rather than by declaring a bound in advance.

The work item that reached the limit does *not* count within the capacity. So the
learned capacity is one _less_ than the number of items in flight when the
failure arrived: the item that hit the wall is the probe that found the edge, not
a member of the set that fits. Capacity is therefore the largest set that all
succeeded — a measurement, not a policy.

That makes the *observability* of a limit the thing that matters, because a limit
teaches nothing unless it reports. A thread that cannot be spawned fails with
`ThreadQuotaExceeded`, naming the limit; an address-space cap fails the
allocation with a real `ENOMEM`; a cgroup counts the kill in `memory.events`
after the fact. A bound that ends in a `SIGKILL` with no counter — which is what
overcommit produces — teaches nothing at all, which is why the sensors above are
what make this measurable.

## What may live where

*The server* owns the process group id, the cgroup, the socket, the registry of
running prompts, the database — the turns and their state, in memory and on disk — and the
tools. It outlives everything else, so it holds what must not be lost. It can pick
up tools added while it is running, without a restart; how that is done is
implementation detail and does not belong here. A tool that returns — with an
error or with a value — produces a result that becomes a turn for the running prompt; the
only case this document has to reason about is a tool that crashes the process,
and see `## What a tool can do to the process`.

*The client* owns the UI and the extensions. An extension never reaches the
server: the client applies it and sends the resulting turn modifications over the
socket, so what crosses is the effect, not the extension. A shared-memory path
between the two would be an optimisation, not a requirement — the socket is the
interface of record.

The client is disposable, and stays disposable because nothing durable lives only
in it. The turns are the server's, and a client's modifications land there, so a
client that dies takes nothing with it but its own unsent edits.

## What a tool can do to the process

A tool that returns — with an error or with a value — produces a result, and the
result becomes a turn for the running prompt. There is nothing to isolate in that: the
result is data, and whether it says error is the running prompt's business.

The one case this document has to reason about is a tool that *crashes the
process* — a panic, a segmentation fault, an abort. That takes the server with it,
and no care at the call site recovers from it.

So the question is not how to contain a crash but how likely one is, and the
answer is the type system. While the checks are on, a tool has no null
dereference, no out-of-bounds index, no silently wrapping integer, no uninitialised
read and no access to an inactive union field, so the silent-corruption class is
gone and most memory bugs become a loud panic instead.

Four things bound how far that reaches:

- *A panic is terminal.* `std.debug.FullPanic` lowers every safety check to a
  `noreturn` call (`outOfBounds`, `unwrapError`, `sentinelMismatch`,
  `inactiveUnionField`, and the rest), so a handler can log and exit but cannot
  resume. The type system gives detection, not containment.
- *The guarantees are the checks.* `ReleaseFast` and `ReleaseSmall` remove
  runtime safety, and the bug that panicked in `ReleaseSafe` becomes silent
  wrongness again. Relying on the type system means shipping the binary that
  hosts tools as `safe` — or `debug`.
- *Types bound neither time nor quantity.* An endless loop and an unbounded
  allocation are both untouched by the type system, and Zig cannot kill a thread
  to end the first. A capped allocator can bound the second; nothing in the
  language bounds the first.
- *The guarantee is a property of the code, not the language.* `@ptrFromInt`, an
  `unreachable` on a path the model can steer, or a single
  `@setRuntimeSafety(false)` anywhere in a tool reopens the hole, so the tool API
  has to keep those out of reach.

What makes a rare crash affordable is where turns live (`research/turns.dj`): a
server that dies loses the in-flight turn, not the session, because the session is
already durable. That is what turns "loud and fatal" into something a fleet can
live with.

## KiTTY

Read from the clone at `~/computing/graphical/kitty`. Kitty is a terminal
emulator, so its job is the opposite of this one — it *isolates* every child —
but the shape of its process set is the worked example above.

*Roles.*

- *The boss* (`kitty/boss.py`, 4137 lines) — one per kitty instance, owning every
  OS window and tab, the GPU context, the sockets, and the child monitor.
- *A child* — a shell or program, one per window or tab, `fork`ed in
  `kitty/child.c` and given its own session, process group and PTY with
  `setsid()` followed by `ioctl(TIOCSCTTY)`.
- *A child's descendants* — whatever is run inside a window; kitty reads their
  cwd, cmdline, environ, memory and process groups, but does not own them.
- *A kitten* — a separate program, of which `kitten @` is a client of a boss.

The child monitor is not a process: `child-monitor.c` runs its `io_loop` and
`talk_loop` as `pthread_create` threads inside the boss, which is why no seventh
process handles `SIGCHLD`.

*Start modes, and the role each lands in.*

- *The launcher* (`kitty/launcher/main.c`) is the `kitty` binary, and it does not
  linger: it parses the command line, sets up the socket, and `execv`s into the
  Python program (`:358`), so the launcher *becomes* the boss.
- *`--detach`* — becomes a boss. The launcher forks; the parent waits on a pipe
  until the child has called `setsid()` and then exits, so the child is not killed
  by a `SIGHUP` when the parent's terminal goes away (`:474-492`). stdin goes to
  `/dev/null`, stdout and stderr to `--detached-log` or `/dev/null`.
- *`--start-as=hidden`* — becomes a boss whose first OS window is hidden: a
  server.
- *`-1` / `--single-instance`* — with a boss already running this does *not*
  become one: the launcher connects to the boss's socket, sends
  `{'cmd': 'new_instance', 'args': […], 'environ': …}`, and exits. With no boss
  running, it becomes the boss.
- *`kitty --replay-commands`* — `kitty/client.py`, which despite the name is a
  replayer of a `--dump-commands` log and no kind of client at all.

*The single-instance socket* (`kitty/launcher/single-instance.c`) is an abstract
unix socket on Linux and a file elsewhere, created under a lock file with mode
0600, with the peer uid verified at *both* ends because abstract sockets are
visible to every process on the box. The connected fd reaches the Python process
through the environment as `KITTY_SI_DATA`, and the client may bind a second
socket so the boss can tell it when the window it opened dies
(`notify_on_os_window_death`).

*Signals* are `SIGINT, SIGHUP, SIGTERM, SIGCHLD, SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2`, masked in
every thread and consumed from one `signalfd`.

*Capacity* is declared rather than learned, which is the opposite of harness1's
rule: `#define MAX_CHILDREN 512` (`kitty/data-types.h:157`) sizes the child
array, the pollfd array, the GPU buffers and the OS-window count, and adding one
past it is refused outright.

## tmux and Herdr

Read from `man tmux` on this machine and from the Herdr checkout at
`~/computing/terminal/herdr`. Both are multiplexers with a long-lived server and
disposable clients, which is the model harness1 is taking.

*tmux.* The server socket lives in a directory under `TMUX_TMPDIR`, or `/tmp`
when that is unset, named `tmux-UID` and required not to be world readable,
writable or executable. `-L socket-name` picks the socket (default `default`),
which is what lets several independent tmux servers run; `-S socket-path` gives a
full path instead and ignores `-L`. If the socket is removed by accident,
`SIGUSR1` to the server recreates it — the server is the owner of the rendezvous,
not a client that happens to hold it.

*Herdr.* A Rust multiplexer for coding agents, with its own server, sessions,
workspaces, tabs and panes. The socket path can be overridden with
`HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`; sessions are named (`herdr session attach <name>`) and
stopped with `herdr server stop`, which is documented as also stopping the pane
processes. Its tests are the architecture in miniature — `server_headless.rs`,
`multi_client.rs`, `detach_reattach.rs`, `live_handoff.rs`, `client_mode.rs` —
and its test helpers decide whether a server is live with exactly the shape above:

```rust
if path.exists() && UnixStream::connect(path).is_ok() {
```

A pane's agent is tracked through a status the server owns — `idle`, `working`,
`blocked`, `done`, `unknown` — which is the state harness1's server needs in order
to know that all running prompts are done and it may exit.

